latest_plan = "Based on the reflection, here's a detailed step-by-step plan to solve the challenge:\n\n1. Find the gray line:\n   - Scan the grid from top to bottom to find a row where all cells are color 5 (gray).\n   - Store the index of this row as the 'gray_line_index'.\n\n2. Initialize variables:\n   - chosen_color = 0 (default to black if no valid line is found)\n   - max_squares_below = 0 (to keep track of the maximum number of squares below the gray line)\n   - rightmost_col = -1 (to handle the tie-breaking rule)\n\n3. Scan the grid from right to left:\n   - For each column, starting from the rightmost and moving left:\n\n     a. Check for a line above the gray line:\n        - Scan upwards from the gray line until you find a non-black color or reach the top.\n        - Store this as 'color_above'.\n\n     b. Check for a line below the gray line:\n        - Scan downwards from the gray line until you find a non-black color or reach the bottom.\n        - Store this as 'color_below'.\n\n     c. If 'color_above' and 'color_below' are the same and not black:\n        - Count the number of squares of this color below the gray line.\n        - Store this count as 'squares_below'.\n\n        - If 'squares_below' > 'max_squares_below', OR\n          if 'squares_below' == 'max_squares_below' and current column > 'rightmost_col':\n          - Update 'chosen_color' to be this color\n          - Update 'max_squares_below' to be 'squares_below'\n          - Update 'rightmost_col' to be the current column\n\n4. Create the output grid:\n   - Create a new 2x2 ColoredGrid\n   - Fill all cells of this grid with the 'chosen_color'\n\n5. Return the output grid\n\nThis plan ensures that:\n- We only consider lines that extend both above and below the gray line.\n- Among those lines, we choose the one with the most squares below the gray line.\n- In case of a tie, we choose the rightmost line (implemented by scanning right-to-left).\n- If no valid line is found, we default to a black (color 0) output grid.\n\nThis approach should work consistently for all the given test cases and any similar inputs adhering to the same pattern."
